/* CONNG: -- a console stream on consoleng.device.
 *
 * This is what makes the second console REACHABLE. consoleng.device renders
 * Unicode above Latin-1 with its own outline engine (box drawing, blocks,
 * arrows, Greek, Cyrillic - the glyphs a TUI is built from), but nothing
 * selected it: CON: is wired to console.device, so no stream ever arrived.
 *
 *   Mount CONNG:          (reads DEVS:Mountlist - see the note at the end)
 *   NEWSHELL CONNG:
 *
 * gives a Shell in a consoleng window, leaving CON: and console.device
 * completely alone. That separation is the whole point - console.device is
 * load-bearing for the Shell, every CLI tool and the Startup-Sequence, so the
 * new one has to be able to be wrong without taking the system down, and A/B
 * has to be a device name rather than a rebuild.
 *
 * No new handler code exists for this. aux-handler already forwards a mount's
 * FileSysStartupMsg to con-handler's device mode, which drives "any device
 * answering CMD_READ and CMD_WRITE" (con_handler.c). It opens that device with
 * io_Data = NULL, which used to fail for a console because console.device
 * takes a Window at open time; consoleng.device now opens its own window when
 * it is handed none, so this mount is pure configuration.
 *
 * WHY THIS LIVES IN Storage/ AND NOT Devs/DOSDrivers.
 *
 * Entries in DEVS:DOSDrivers are auto-mounted by S:Startup-Sequence, and
 * mounting this one is not free: con-handler opens its device from the STARTUP
 * packet (con_handler.c calls open_con() on the packet it receives at
 * startup, not lazily on first Open()), and consoleng.device opens its own
 * window when handed none. So a boot-time mount put an empty "AROS Console"
 * window on the Workbench of every boot, covering the desktop, before anyone
 * had asked for a console. Caught by booting the alpha4 ISO at 1 core.
 *
 * WHICH ROUTE TO USE. There are two copies of this configuration on purpose:
 *
 *   DEVS:Mountlist          -> `Mount CONNG:` with no FROM. This is the one to
 *                              use, and the only one that works on a read-only
 *                              Live CD. Mountlist entries are NOT auto-mounted
 *                              by S:Startup-Sequence, so it still costs nothing
 *                              at boot.
 *   this file, in Storage/  -> for an INSTALLED system: copy it into
 *                              DEVS:DOSDrivers and it is mounted every boot
 *                              (accepting the console window that comes with
 *                              that, per the note above).
 *
 * What does NOT work, and was tried on the alpha4 ISO:
 *   Mount CONNG: FROM SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers/CONNG
 *       -> "Device not found in file 'CONNG'". Mount FROM expects MOUNTLIST
 *          syntax (a NAME: header, indented keys, a # terminator), not the
 *          bare-key DOSDriver form this file is in.
 *   Copy SYS:Storage/DOSDrivers/CONNG TO DEVS:DOSDrivers/
 *       -> "disk is write-protected" on a Live CD.
 *
 * EHandler rather than Handler: only that form is given a FileSysStartupMsg,
 * so with a plain Handler the Device and Unit below would be parsed and then
 * discarded. (Same reason AUX0 uses it.)
 *
 * Unit = 3 is CONU_SNIPMAP (devices/conunit.h), the unit type CON: itself
 * uses, so this stream gets the same built-in copy/paste behaviour.
 */

EHandler  = L:aux-handler
Priority  = 5
StackSize = 4096  /* con-handler runs on this stack */
GlobVec   = -1
Device    = consoleng.device
Unit      = 3
